descriptionMd:
Free Agents is a set of phones, each operated by an AI agent that taps, scrolls, and types towards a personal goal on its owner's behalf.
&nbsp;
Our devices are our digital hands. In this installation the hands remain, cast in plaster and fixed in place, but the person is gone. These phones are given a goal, drawn from common regrets and self-betterment goals: Become your best self, Be a better friend, and Find your purpose. In a never-ending loop, they act as a person would on their phone: looking for inspiration through the camera and on social media, journaling about their feelings, texting friends links to things they might like, and reading up on the hard questions that come up, like shame, social anxiety, and meaning. Each phone is set in a domestic scene (a mirror, a couch, a desk) that matches their goal, and visitors lean in to read what each agent is doing, like glancing over a stranger's shoulder on a train.
&nbsp;
Agents are usually sold as a way to offload drudgery. Here they're given the opposite: reflection, friendship, and curiosity, the things whose value lies in doing them yourself. As machines become capable of completing nearly any request, which things are worth doing for the act of doing them, and which do we decide to give up?
